uld narrow the margin, though closing an 18-point gap would require a shift far beyond any recent cycle.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Idaho remains one of the most reliably Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly 18 points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is an open contest, since the incumbent president is term-limited, and neither side gains an edge from incumbency. No public polls of the state or expert race ratings are available, so the outlook rests on Idaho's long-standing voting pattern.\n\nRecent headlines offer little to suggest a change in the state's trajectory; national attention has centered on the 2028 primaries and races elsewhere. Barring an extraordinary national environment, the Republican nominee starts with a commanding advantage in Idaho's four electoral votes. The Democratic path to competitiveness would require a shift far larger than any recent presidential cycle has produced in the state.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0246, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0539, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0537, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0553, "margin": -18.0}]}